1.text:
The flag of Tonga consists of a solid red field, a white canton occupying the upper hoist side, and a red Greek cross centered within the canton. An image of the flag at any finite resolution therefore yields a matrix whose entries take only the two values \(a\) (red) and \(b\) (white).  

Because the design is composed of axis-aligned rectangular regions of constant color, every row of the matrix is piecewise constant, with breakpoints occurring only at the vertical boundaries of the canton and of the cross. Consequently there are at most five combinatorially distinct row vectors:

- entirely red (rows below the canton);
- white on the left, red on the right (rows inside the canton but outside the cross);
- three further patterns produced by the horizontal arm of the cross (the central red segment appears in three different vertical positions relative to the canton).

These five vectors live in a vector space whose dimension is at most three: each is an affine combination of the two indicator vectors of the horizontal and vertical divisions induced by the canton and cross. Equivalently, after the change of variables \(M=(a-b)A+b\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top\), the 0-1 matrix \(A\) that marks the red regions is itself a sum of three rank-1 matrices (outer products of the indicator vectors of the rectangular blocks). Hence \(\operatorname{rank}(M)\le3\) for any choice of \(a,b\) and any resolution.

The bound is attained: when \(a\neq b\) the three rank-1 summands are linearly independent, so matrices realizing rank exactly 3 exist. Therefore the maximal possible rank is 3.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {3 (attained by any sufficiently resolved rasterization with a≠b)}

